<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mary of Burgundy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1457">1457</a>–<a href="/years/1482">1482</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Mary the Rich</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> was the only child of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> and heiress of the Burgundian State. When her father died in 1477, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI of France</a> seized the duchy of Burgundy, and she married Maximilian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> to defend the rest. Her early death after a riding accident left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> and Franche-Comté to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mary-of-burgundy.jpg" alt="Mary of Burgundy" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Possibly Michael Pacher · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mary_of_Burgundy_(1458%E2%80%931482),_by_Netherlandish_or_South_German_School_of_the_late_15th_Century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Through the marriage of <b>Mary of Burgundy</b> to Maximilian of Austria in 1477, Flanders passed to the Habsburgs and became one of the provinces of the Habsburg Netherlands and then of the Spanish Netherlands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Hainaut became part of the Burgundian Netherlands, passed to the Habsburgs on the death of <b>Mary of Burgundy</b> in 1482, and under Charles V and Philip II belonged to the Seventeen Provinces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Through the marriage of <b>Mary of Burgundy</b> to Maximilian of Austria, the Burgundian inheritance and the claim to the duchy passed to the Habsburgs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis XI seized the duchy; <b>Mary of Burgundy</b> married Maximilian of Austria, and after her death in 1482 the Treaty of Arras split the inheritance, the Low Countries passing to the Habsburg Netherlands.</blockquote><a href="/state/burgundian-state">Burgundian State</a></li><li><blockquote>Through marriage it passed to Philip the Bold in 1384 and joined the Burgundian State, then went with <b>Mary of Burgundy</b> to the Habsburgs and the Spanish crown.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-county-of-burgundy">Free County of Burgundy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mary of Burgundy's</b> death in 1482 left the Burgundian State's northern lands to her infant son Philip the Handsome, with her widower Maximilian of Austria as regent.</blockquote><a href="/state/habsburg-netherlands">Habsburg Netherlands</a></li></ul></section>
